     1|/**
     2| * Owner-gated password update that bypasses Supabase AAL2 enforcement.
     3| *
     4| * Root cause this fixes: when the Supabase project-level auth config has
     5| * `mfa_allow_low_aal: false`, the client-side `supabase.auth.updateUser({ password })`
     6| * call rejects AAL1 sessions with "AAL2 session is required to update email or
     7| * password when MFA is enabled" — even when the owner has 0 MFA factors enrolled.
     8| * The Supabase Management API refuses to flip `mfa_allow_low_aal`, so the only
     9| * in-app fix is to route the password update through the service-role admin API,
    10| * which is not subject to AAL enforcement.
    11| *
    12| * Security contract (NOT a hole):
    13| *   1. Owner bearer verified via assertIVXRegisteredOwnerBearer (real Supabase JWT
    14| *      + owner email allowlist).
    15| *   2. Current password verified against live Supabase
    16| *      `auth/v1/token?grant_type=password` with the anon key BEFORE any admin
    17| *      mutation — proves the caller knows the current password.
    18| *   3. New password validated against the enterprise 12-char policy.
    19| *   4. Password applied via `admin.auth.admin.updateUserById` (service role).
    20| *   5. The owner's current session is preserved (no global signOut) so the owner
    21| *      is not logged out of the app they are using to change the password.
    22| */
